# For each pair, choose ONE label.
# 
#   same      The two submissions make the SAME argument, and the second adds no
#             substantive point the first does not already make. Wording may
#             differ freely. A form letter with a different signature, a different
#             town, or one inserted sentence of personal context is `same`.
# 
#   variant   Clearly derived from a common source, but one of them adds a real
#             substantive point of its own (a new objection, a new piece of
#             evidence, a different requested remedy).
# 
#   distinct  Two independently made arguments, even if they reach the same
#             conclusion. Two people who both say "this rule is too weak" in their
#             own words are DISTINCT: that is public opinion, not a campaign.
# 
#   unusable  One or both texts are too garbled, truncated or empty to judge.
# 
# If you are torn between `same` and `distinct`, choose `distinct`. Merging two
# real arguments erases someone from the public record; splitting a campaign only
# overstates diversity.
# 
# Fill "label" with: same | variant | distinct | unusable
# Do NOT look at labels/machine_labels.json first. The point is independence.
{"pair": 57, "band": "0.80-0.85", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1411", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3665", "a_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, ***Industry cannot claim that natural gas is a valuable resource for which they demand land access, and simultaneously claim license to vent the same gas into the atmosphere, or burn it in the open air. If the industry is not economically motivated to invest in sealing their pipelines, wellheads, and mine shafts properly, and getting that gas into the appropriate conveyance for production, rather than disposing of the resource in situ, then the price of natural gas must not be high enough to grant that industry the access rights for production in the first place. EPA must require all industries and companies, at all stages of the methane supply chain, to incorporate the cost of methane abatement into deciding whether a given project is profitable and in the public interest, or whether methane is actually a financially desirable energy source for a given purpose. Just as electricity utilities consider smog or particulate pollution control costs in choosing whether to initiate, maintain, or close power plants, so must everyone who transports, produces, or consumes methane make a similar legally\u2010required assessment, not use our atmosphere as a complimentary dumping ground under the preposterous illusion that nobody will notice.*** As an American, concerned about climate change and air pollution, I strongly support EPA\u2019s proposed rule to reduce methane and other harmful air pollutants from both new and existing sources in the oil and natural gas industry", "b_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 09000064855ef831 - Joanna Nix - United States - - <![CDATA[ My name is Joanna, and I live in Northampton, Massachusetts. I\u2019m writing to express support for the updated draft rule to reduce methane pollution from oil and gas wells. The harmful environmental and health effects of this extremely potent greenhouse gas are well documented, and it I would like to thank the EPA for its responsiveness to concerns from environmental and clean energy advocates expressed during the first open comment period. In particular, the inclusion of fugitive methane monitoring for small wells was an important gap to address from the previous draft rule. I encourage the EPA to strengthen requirements for community involvement in state implementation of the rule, and to require methane inspection data to be made publicly available. This will ensure proper accountability on behalf of all stakeholders on an issue that can have far A truly robust methane pollution standard would ban routine gas flaring by the oil industry and limit flaring to only highly specific cases for which there is no alternative. We\u2019re at a critical moment for capping planet-warming emissions before we see the worst effects of climate change. The proposed draft rule, combined with the methane fee recently passed in the Inflation Reduction Act, is essential for avoiding those tipping points. We're already feeling the devastating impacts of climate change. I lived in California for a few years, and poor air quality "}
{"pair": 55, "band": "0.80-0.85", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2475", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2558", "a_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, Methane pollution from the oil and gas sector is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and communities across the country. That is why we are counting on the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to quickly finalize the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane and other harmful pollution from the oil and gas industry. The updated draft rule improves upon or maintained many of the standards in the original draft rule released by EPA in 2021 identified by climate, public health, and environmental justice advocates like me, including by ensuring all wells are regularly and frequently inspected for leaks and that outdated pneumatic equipment is replaced with zero-emitting options. But there is more work to be done to ensure that communities like mine are protected. EPA must do more to ensure that: - Operators at wells capture associated gas and limit flaring of that gas in instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons; - Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making the standards applicable to more tanks; and - Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program by ensuring communities and citizens can access data and technologies to help reduce methane and other pollutants in the areas where we live and work. My community is counting on your agency to quickly address these concerns, and to finalize strong, comprehensive new an", "b_text": "From: Jim Steitz < > To: A-AND-R-DOCKET Date: 2/7/2023 7:20:01 PM Subject: Methane Waste is Gross Resource Misconduct --------------------------------------------------- ***EPA\u2019s defense of the habitability of our planet cannot be circumscribed to what industry finds financially convenient, or allow the persistence of every chemical and industrial process conceived by a business concern. EPA must stop this outrageous charade and require that all methane pried from the ground is used or otherwise kept out of the atmosphere, an elementary criterion of sanity in how we handle our planet\u2019s dangerous hydrocarbon reservoirs. Any concessions to industry interests of convenience or cost in venting or flaring methane would do outrageous violence to our ethical obligations to our children, and their right to a habitable atmosphere.*** Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additi"}
{"pair": 51, "band": "0.80-0.85", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0509", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2477", "a_text": "Dear EPA Oil & Gas Docket, We live on one planet - it should remain habitable for future generations. We need to reverse the damage that we have done to earth\u2019s atmosphere, natural habitats and ecosystems. We should maintain/enact better environmental and wilderness preservation standards going forward. Much too slowly, we are realizing the importance of how what we do (and don\u2019t do) effects our environment. I second: \u201cAs a parent and member of Moms Clean Air Force, I\u2019m deeply concerned with the health impacts of air pollution and climate change. That\u2019s why I was encouraged to learn that the EPA has proposed rules to cut methane and other harmful air pollutants from new and existing oil and gas operations. This is an important first step to address the impacts of methane pollution. That\u2019s why I urge you to ensure that these rules are strong and comprehensive. They must cover all sources of oil and gas operations including smaller, leak-prone wells and limit routine flaring, both of which are outsized sources of methane emissions.. Quickly and significantly reducing methane pollution is one of the best levers we have to slow the rate of climate change now, and it will help clean up the air to protect children\u2019s health. Federal methane rules will establish vital protections for all children across the nation, especially for states that have failed to enact meaningful oil and gas methane protections at the state level. Please ensure that the EPA passes comprehensive methane rule", "b_text": "February 13, 2023 The Honorable Michael S. Regan Administrator of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency USEPA William Jefferson Clinton Building 1200 Pennsylvania Avenue N.W. Washington, DC 20004 Ref: Dear Administrator Regan, Thank you for the opportunity to give my comments on the methane supplemental rule referenced above. As a pro-life evangelical, I have a special concern for the unborn. I want children to be born healthy and unhindered by the ravages of pollution even before they take their first breath. The medical community has long known that unborn children are especially vulnerable to environmental impacts. Of these impacts, fossil fuels are the most serious threat to children\u2019s health worldwide. All of us would agree that our children are precious and must be defended from the threats imposed by leaking poisons emitted from oil/gas production, distribution, and transportation. These include known cancer-causing toxics like benzene, VOCs that increase ozone levels, and methane. Methane is an over 80 times more potent greenhouse gas than CO2 in the first twenty years and a major contributor to global warming and climate-fueled extreme weather. Rising temperatures not only pose the threat of heat illness and death during heat waves, but also directly contribute to worsening ozone levels. The medical and scientific literature is clear \u2013 living within 0.5 miles of a methane extraction or production site harms our children, and newer research suggests that even those"}
{"pair": 58, "band": "0.80-0.85", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1923", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2092", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you in advance for your efforts to reduce methane emissions from the fossil fuel industries. I call on you to strengthen your proposal to reduce the oil and gas industry\u2019s methane pollution. Sincerely, Debbie Weaver Durham, NC 27713 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/9871.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:29 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, I hope that you will continue to act to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. We need you to act in every way you can to and strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. We all know that the climate crisis is wreaking untold damage on people, animals and environment in the US and all over the world from the outputs of the toxic, polluting oil and gas industry who are lobbying to increase their already ill- gotten profits. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Rhoda Schlamm Woodside, NY 11377 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/4026.txt[2/1/2023 2:27:28 PM]"}
{"pair": 47, "band": "0.80-0.85", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1412", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3226", "a_text": "Perez, JuanB From: Sent: To: Subject: Barbara Brandom < > Sunday, December 5, 2021 8:21 PM A-AND-R-DOCKET Here is the link to Cesar Aguirre's presentation at the 2021 Shale Gas and Public Health Conference. This is the source of my comment about his work in my testimony to your EPA committee about improved methane rules. It is a great example of what community organizing can do to improve public health. Every state needs this type of work. Please let me know if I may provide more information for you. Sincerely, BW Brandom, MD (retired) tel # 814\u2010926\u20103075 Concerned Health Professionals of PA, Steering Committee member Climate Reality member On Sun, Dec 5, 2021 at 8:10 PM Barbara Brandom < > wrote: In response to your request for more information. This is a link to the 2021 Shale Gas and Public Health Conference, produced by the LWV PA. Cesar Aguirre, CCEJN, is the speaker whose work I mentioned in my testimony. This link includes Cesar's response to questions. I will look for another link to his presentation. Thank you for your interest, BW Brandom, MD On Thu, Dec 2, 2021 at 7:51 PM Barbara Brandom < > wrote: Below I paste my testimony. Today I am sitting in Somerset County PA, on land that was once inhabited by the Monongahela People. I am a 71 year old grandmother and retired physician. For 35 years I worked as a pediatric anesthesiologist. Thus my training and strongest motivation is to protect our children. It is already the case that if our energy industries in my home st", "b_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 09000064855d6786 - Betty Ramsey - - <![CDATA[ Dear EPA Administrator Regan, Methane pollution from the oil and gas sector is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming our health and the climate. That is why we are counting on the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to quickly finalize the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane and other harmful pollution from the oil and gas industry. The updated draft rule improves upon or maintained many of the standards in the original draft rule released by EPA in 2021 identified by climate, public health, and environmental justice advocates like me, including by ensuring all wells are regularly and frequently monitored for leaks and that outdated pneumatic equipment is replaced with zero EPA must do more to ensure that: \u00b7 Operators at wells capture associated gas and limit flaring of that gas to instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons; \u00b7 Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making the standards applicable to more tanks; and \u00b7 Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program by ensuring communities and citizens can access data and technologies to help reduce methane and other pollutants in the areas where we live and work. My community is counting on your agency to quickly address these concerns, and to finalize strong, comprehensive new and existing source rules to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas sector be"}
{"pair": 65, "band": "0.85-0.90", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1923", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1928", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you in advance for your efforts to reduce methane emissions from the fossil fuel industries. I call on you to strengthen your proposal to reduce the oil and gas industry\u2019s methane pollution. Sincerely, Debbie Weaver Durham, NC 27713 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/9871.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:29 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Therese MacKenzie Haverford, PA 19041 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/15582.txt[1/23/2023 11:56:43 AM]"}
{"pair": 79, "band": "0.85-0.90", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1923", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2505", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you in advance for your efforts to reduce methane emissions from the fossil fuel industries. I call on you to strengthen your proposal to reduce the oil and gas industry\u2019s methane pollution. Sincerely, Debbie Weaver Durham, NC 27713 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/9871.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:29 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, You must take action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. It is already late and we are at the final hours. But , something can still be done to save our planet. Being strict on Methane emissions is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Timothy Biel New York, NY 10065 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/7472.txt[2/21/2023 2:33:08 PM]"}
{"pair": 74, "band": "0.85-0.90", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2041", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2217", "a_text": "From: Becca Phillips < > Sent: Friday, January 13, 2023 9:10 AM To: Nanishka Albaladejo < > Subject: Re: EPA Public Hearing-Supplemental Proposal for the Oil & Natural Gas Industry-Zoom Link: Day2 Session3 Hello, Attached are my comments from the testimony I provided at one of the hearings on Wednesday evening. Thank you! Hello all, EPA Methane Rule - 1.13.23 Testimony Becca Phillips, Climate and Health Fellow at PSR Thank you for giving me the time to speak today. My name is Becca Phillips and I am currently an MPH student and the climate and health fellow at Physicians for Social Responsibility where I run a climate and health Ambassador program for young and emerging health professionals. I am speaking today to urge the EPA to pass this proposed rule to reduce leaks of methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. As a young person and emerging public health practitioner also speaking on behalf of the young health professionals I work with, we are deeply fearful, anxious, and concerned about climate change as a threat to our health and future on a liveable planet. Everyday on the news and in my inbox I see stories of flooding, fires, increased zoonotic disease transmission, air pollution, heat waves, harmful algal blooms, and extreme weather events due to climate change that are causing deep suffering, mass displacement, loss, and trauma to so many people and other species. We cannot treat our patients, care for our communities, and i", "b_text": "Administrator Michael Regan Environmental Protection Agency WJC Building South 1200 Pennsylvania Avenue NW Washington, DC 20004 February 9, 2023 Re: Standards of Performance for New, Reconstructed, and Modified Sources and Emissions Guidelines for Existing Sources: Oil and Natural Gas Sector Climate Review Docket Number EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317 Dear Administrator Regan, On behalf of the Union for Reform Judaism, whose nearly 850 congregations across North America include 1.8 million Reform Jews, and the Central Conference of Americans Rabbis, whose membership includes more than 2,000 Reform rabbis, I write in response to the proposed rule entitled \u201cStandards of Performance for New, Reconstructed, and Modified Sources and Emissions Guidelines for Existing Sources: Oil and Natural Gas Sector Climate Review\u201d (RIN 2060-AV16). We are deeply concerned about methane emissions, their harmful contributions to climate change, and the negative health impacts on Americans, particularly Americans living in frontline communities. The climate crisis is an ongoing calamity, causing rising sea levels, global temperature extremes, and increases in severe weather, wildfires, and droughts. These climate impacts are detrimental to human health and wellbeing, particularly and disproportionately felt by vulnerable Communities of Color. As you know, methane is especially detrimental to global and human health. Despite its shorter lifespan, its chemical composition makes it more effective at trapping car"}
{"pair": 68, "band": "0.85-0.90", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2727", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3747", "a_text": "Dear Administrator Michael Regan, ***EPA\u2019s defense of the habitability of our planet cannot be circumscribed to what industry finds financially convenient, or allow the persistence of every chemical and industrial process conceived by a business concern. EPA must stop this outrageous charade and require that all methane pried from the ground is used or otherwise kept out of the atmosphere, an elementary criterion of sanity in how we handle our planet\u2019s dangerous hydrocarbon reservoirs. Any concessions to industry interests of convenience or cost in venting or flaring methane would do outrageous violence to our ethical obligations to our children, and their right to a habitable atmosphere.*** Methane pollution from the oil and gas sector is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and communities across the country. That is why we are counting on the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to quickly finalize the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane and other harmful pollution from the oil and gas industry. The updated draft rule improves upon or maintains many of the standards proposed by EPA in 2021 and identified by climate, public health, and environmental justice advocates like me, including by ensuring all wells are regularly and frequently monitored for leaks and that outdated pneumatic equipment is replaced with zero-emitting options. In addition to ensuring that each of these key provisions remain in the final rule, there is", "b_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 090000648568e5b8 - Tess Dornfeld - Grand Marais MN United States 55604 - - <![CDATA[ I am writing to tell EPA to adopt the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane emissions as well as other harmful pollution from the oil and gas industry, as quickly as possible. I appreciate that the updated draft rule improves on or maintains many of the standards in the original 2021 draft rule, including by ensuring all wells are regularly and frequently inspected and monitored for leaks, and that outdated pneumatic equipment is replaced with zero In addition to ensuring that these key provisions remains in the final rule, EPA must do more to address methane pollution at the scale of the threat. Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is one of the quickest, most cost Here in Minnesota, we have had two summers of historic drought - in 2021, our northern forests looked like September or October, in the middle of July Our economy is already suffering, as well. I worked for over a decade as a seasonal crewmember at a Boundary Waters outfitter. In 2021, the entire Wilderness was closed for the first time in its 60+ years of existence, because there were so many wildfires, it was impossible for responders to keep up. Fortunately no lives were lost that year, but many people lost homes, and the lost business continues today, as entry permit numbers had to be reduced. Thousands of direct and indirect jobs and livelihoods depend on outdoor recreation in this regi"}
{"pair": 64, "band": "0.85-0.90", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1635", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3455", "a_text": "Submitted on December 20, 2022 11:30 am EST Submitted by: Anonymous Submitted values are: Name: Joseph Adams Email Address: Comments: Regarding EPA\u2019s proposed rule to reduce methane emissions by the oil and natural gas industry, I am the EPA to enact stronger safeguards so that release of methane (and other gasses) does not continue to *undermine* the progress we *appear* to be making in reducing greenhouse gas emissions. We need to build upon EPA's 2021 rule to reduce methane pollution from the oil and gas industry, which was an important step but needs to go farther. EPA must strengthen this rule by - Making methane standards applicable to more storage tanks; and - Ensuring that citizens are able to access data and technologies to reduce methane their communities, through more accessible participation in the Super Emitter Response Program. - Ensuring that oil wells must capture gas and limit gas flaring. THANK YOU for your consideration.", "b_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 090000648561302f - Denis Galvin - Mclean Fairfax, Virginia 22101 170-384-7023 - - <![CDATA[ I am a former Deputy Director of the National Park Service. It is clear that climate change is the most serious threat to these cultural icons in their history. In your proposal to regulate emissions from oil and gas extraction on public I advocate for the strongest protection resulting in minimal emissions for these operations. Such regulation will protect not only our national parks, but also our communities and public health. As you know, methane is a powerful greenhouse gas and is often leaked and vented during oil and gas operations, degrading air quality, driving climate change, threatening public health, and harming the natural conditions of our national parks. While this updated supplemental rule improves upon the standards previously proposed in November 2021, there are three key aspects that need to be strengthened before EPA finalizes the proposal: 1. Completely eliminate the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities. 2. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making the standards applicable to more tanks. 3. Ensure that approved monitoring technologies and data are accessible to all so that communities and individuals can fully engage in the proposed Super Emitter Response Program. Thank you for your continued attention to curbing methane emissions, something we must do quickly if we are to stem fu"}
{"pair": 92, "band": "0.90-0.95", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1166", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1248", "a_text": "As a chemist, I am painfully aware that methane in the atmosphere is a vast source of global warming. Methane needs to regulated to give as low releases as can possibly be managed. Im writing to urge EPA to strengthen the proposed emissions regulations for new and existing oil and gas industry sources of methane pollution. EPA must take this opportunity to slash emissions of this potent greenhouse gas to limit the devastating impacts of climate change in the coming decades while also helping communities near these facilities that suffer from toxic air pollution. EPA should require the oil and gas industry to limit the amount of methane that escapes into our atmosphere by adopting strict leak detection and repair requirements even for low-producing wells. EPA should also limit the use of venting and flaring in non-emergency situations. The methane reductions a stronger final rule will achieve are crucial to the U.S. achieving President Bidens climate goals and to keeping the promises made in the Paris Agreement.", "b_text": "Im writing to urge EPA to strengthen the proposed emissions regulations for new and existing oil and gas industry sources of methane pollution. EPA must take this opportunity to slash emissions of this potent greenhouse gas to limit the devastating impacts of climate change in the coming decades while also helping communities near these facilities that suffer from toxic air pollution. EPA should require the oil and gas industry to limit the amount of methane that escapes into our atmosphere by adopting strict leak detection and repair requirements even for low-producing wells. EPA should also limit the use of venting and flaring in non-emergency situations. The methane reductions a stronger final rule will achieve are crucial to the U.S. achieving President Bidens climate goals and to keeping the promises made in the Paris Agreement. YOU are the agency which has the power to strongly impact reduction of methane emissions. Please, for the welfare of all humans, use that power to get results!!!"}
{"pair": 95, "band": "0.90-0.95", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1897", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2142", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Time is running out to act since the Republicans have taken the House. You know they will look at cutting your budget and may try to eliminate the EPA. Act now while you can. Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, STEVE MORRELL Hartford, CT 06103 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/13565.txt[1/23/2023 11:55:55 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, but it's not enough. I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. We have very little time now to blunt the impact of the climate crisis and dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to increase our odds of surviving this catastrophe we are bringing down upon ourselves. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Anne-Christine Strugnell San Rafael, CA 94903 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/2558.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:19 PM]"}
{"pair": 91, "band": "0.90-0.95", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1827", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3148", "a_text": "Dear Administrator Michael Regan, Methane pollution from the oil and gas extraction, production, storage, and use is accelerating the pace of polluting greenhouse gas emissions and climate change. This is clearly harming the health of all families and communities across the USA and elsewhere. The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) must quickly finalize the strongest, most thorough and appropriate safeguards to cut and end methane along with all other harmful pollution from the outdated and obsolete oil and gas industry. The updated draft rule improves upon or maintains many of the crucial standards proposed by EPA in 2021, which have also been identified by public health, climate and environmental justice advocates. This includes ensuring that all wells are regularly and frequently monitored for unavoidable methane leaks, and followed up by routine preventative and corrective maintenance, repaired, updated, shut down/capped, cleaned-up, etc. It must be ensured that outdated pneumatic equipment is replaced with zero-emitting options. In addition to ensuring that each of these key provisions remain in the final rule, there is more work to be done to ensure that all communities, such as my family's, are protected from toxic methane pollution. EPA must do much more to ensure that: *Operators at wells capture associated gas, and limit the toxic fumes of the flaring of captured gas in instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons; *Strengthen the standards", "b_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 0900006485656678 - Elizabeth Riebschlaeger - Victoria TX United States 77901 - - <![CDATA[ EPA Administrator Michael Regan, Dear EPA Administrator Regan, It is established by credible environmental scientists that methane (natural gas) is 83-86 times more destructive to our protective ozone layer than is CO2. Saying that it is a safer, clean energy resources is therefore deceptive advertising. Methane is accelerating Global Warming and causing the growing number, larger and more powerful natural disasters like hurricanes. Billions in financial losses and human misery are the results. Methane pollution from the oil and gas sector is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and communities across the country. That is why I\u2019m asking you to quickly finalize the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane and other harmful pollution, including emissions associated with health impacts like cancer and childhood asthma, from the oil and gas industry. I\u2019m writing in support of the provisions in the updated rule that ensure all wells are regularly monitored for leaks and that outdated pneumatic equipment is replaced with zero Although the methane rule takes important steps to reduce climate-warming methane pollution, there is much that can be done to better protect communities that are threatened by oil & gas pollution. EPA must do more to ensure that: - Operators at wells capture associated gas and limit flaring of that gas in insta"}
{"pair": 87, "band": "0.90-0.95", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1928", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2132", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Therese MacKenzie Haverford, PA 19041 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/15582.txt[1/23/2023 11:56:43 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Climate change is the most important issue to me, and as such, I appreciate your action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. This is a big step in the right direction, and I urge you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. The people hit the hardest are those who suffer the most from structural racism and other structural inequities. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis, along with the imbalanced impacts communities and individuals across the country and world are feeling. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Sarah Mazze Eugene, OR 97404 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/9791.txt[2/1/2023 2:29:00 PM]"}
{"pair": 86, "band": "0.90-0.95", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1973", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2141", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for continuing to take action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your strengthening of the November 2021 proposal makes progress in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules even further to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and the most recent scientific reports make it clear that people across the United States - from heat waves to wildfires to severe drought to devastating changes on wildlife habitat - are feeling the impact in every state. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today since methane by weight is so much more devastating in its contributions to greenhouse gas emissions than carbon dioxide. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Patricia Brennan Providence, RI 02906 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/8735.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:04 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Especially in a context where it's increasingly clear that that the oil and gas industry has known for decades how harmful their emissions would be, where they have spent inordinate amounts of money misleading and greenwashing, and where they have exploited all of us for record profits, please further strengthen your efforts to require them to reduce methane emissions. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Gary Fredericksen Olympia, WA 98502 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/2515.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:08 PM]"}
{"pair": 100, "band": "0.95-0.99", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1846", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2088", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. We need more immediate action to save the planet. We can and should lead the way for the rest of the world. Thanks for your attention/Paula Holmes The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Paula Holmes Clifton, VA 20124 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/4382.txt[1/23/2023 11:52:02 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Arlene Gawne Las Vegas, NV 89134 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/3423.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:47 PM]"}
{"pair": 112, "band": "0.95-0.99", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1847", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2048", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction. Please strengthen the final rules to maximize methane emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating. people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions that the EPA can take to begin stemming the climate crisis. Thank you for your action to cut methane emissions. Please continue to strengthen and implement common-sense regulations. Sincerely, Edward Main Houston, TX 77098 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/4725.txt[1/23/2023 11:52:05 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I hope you will strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating, and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest impact actions we can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration of this matter, and for your actions to cut methane emissions. Sincerely, Jerry DiMarco Bozeman, MT 59771 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/9170.txt[2/1/2023 2:28:42 PM]"}
{"pair": 118, "band": "0.95-0.99", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1945", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2096", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. Please stop the race to climate catastrophe as weather related disasters are increasing at an alarming rate and destabilizing working families and the vulnerable. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Anita Amstutz Albuquerque, NM 87108 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/14689.txt[1/23/2023 11:56:20 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, The American people STRONGLY URGE the EPA to take MUCH, MUCH STRONGER ACTIONS for the ENVIRONMENT and CLIMATE!!!!! I'm glad you want to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. I IMPLORE YOU TO STRENGTHEN the final rules to maximize emissions reductions!!!!! ACT PROMPTLY AND STRONGLY!!!!! The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Glen Anderson Lacey, WA 98503 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...vio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/10346.txt[2/1/2023 2:29:22 PM]"}
{"pair": 108, "band": "0.95-0.99", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2142", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2640", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, but it's not enough. I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. We have very little time now to blunt the impact of the climate crisis and dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to increase our odds of surviving this catastrophe we are bringing down upon ourselves. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Anne-Christine Strugnell San Rafael, CA 94903 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/2558.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:19 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, This is to thank your agency for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Please consider strengthening the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. So many of us are concerned, and afraid, of the accelerating climate crisis that is impacting this country and the entire world. I understand a large reduction in methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions that the government can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Julia Herd New York, NY 10025 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/7176.txt[2/21/2023 2:32:59 PM]"}
{"pair": 106, "band": "0.95-0.99", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1832", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2105", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Since methane emissions are the 2nd most polluting gases that we see its time to set more stringent rule and enforcement to gain control and slow the heating up of out planet. Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Roger Kern Scotts Valley, CA 95066 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/1628.txt[1/23/2023 11:51:12 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. You have taken a significant step in the right direction. I now strongly urge that you strengthen the final rules to maximize -- and make as immediate as possible -- emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and We the People are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. As a progressively-minded voter, I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, R D Frankel Cathedral City, CA 92234 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/3202.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:41 PM]"}
{"pair": 128, "band": "0.99-1.01", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1971", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1992", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact - we have felt it tremendously here in Oregon. We went from just 3 days over 90\u2019s here in the mountains to now seeing 3-5 weeks of greater than 90 in the summers. Crazy. And we had to evacuate this summer due to a wildfire that spread towards us in 3 days from originally starting 25 miles from us. Imagine that. We are lucky that the winds shifted, other wise our place would be gone. And we have lived with fire up here all along, but the heat & the winds are what has changed. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Nancy Phillips La Pine, OR 97739 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/9159.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:12 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact - we have felt it tremendously here in Oregon. We went from just 3 days over 90\u2019s here in the mountains to now seeing 3-5 weeks of greater than 90 in the summers. Crazy. And we had to evacuate this summer due to a wildfire that spread towards us in 3 days from originally starting 25 miles from us. Imagine that. We are lucky that the winds shifted, other wise our place would be gone. And we have lived with fire up here all along, but the heat & the winds are what has changed. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Nancy Phillips La Pine, OR 97739 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/9159.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:12 AM]"}
{"pair": 137, "band": "0.99-1.01", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3196", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3546", "a_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 09000064856225bf - AJ Cho - United States - - <![CDATA[ I want to thank the EPA for drafting updated methane regulation standards and would like to remind the agency to include the following: Permitting gas flares only for safety or maintenance reasons Strengthened emissions standards for storage tanks Easier, more accessible participation in the Super Emitter Response Program ]]> Web file://prod-rend2k1201/Adlib/DocumentumConnector/Work/879EBE68-8E62-4906-... 4/5/2023", "b_text": "Page 1 of 1 - 09000064856225bf - AJ Cho - United States - - <![CDATA[ I want to thank the EPA for drafting updated methane regulation standards and would like to remind the agency to include the following: Permitting gas flares only for safety or maintenance reasons Strengthened emissions standards for storage tanks Easier, more accessible participation in the Super Emitter Response Program ]]> Web file://prod-rend2k1201/Adlib/DocumentumConnector/Work/8071B892-DBEA-469E... 4/5/2023"}
{"pair": 130, "band": "0.99-1.01", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2150", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2159", "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. One key to success with your efforts rests with effective auditing and enforcement mechanisms for methane reduction. Please make sure that these activities are thoroughly addressed. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, John Brubaker Naperville, IL 60540 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/2822.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:28 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. One key to success with your efforts rests with effective auditing and enforcement mechanisms for methane reduction. Please make sure that these activities are thoroughly addressed. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, John Brubaker Naperville, IL 60540 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/2822.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:28 PM]"}
{"pair": 131, "band": "0.99-1.01", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1664", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2831", "a_text": "Dear EPA EPA, I am deeply concerned with the health impacts of air pollution and climate change on my family and my community. I am counting on a strong and comprehensive final methane rule to address the gaps in the rule proposed in 2021. I am encouraged to see that the latest methane proposal includes several improvements over the earlier version, such as - Strengthening leak detection and repair (LDAR) requirements, including requiring inspections at small wells with leak-prone equipment, which are responsible for roughly half of all the oil and gas methane emissions nationwide; - Maintaining zero-emitting pneumatic equipment requirements; - Taking steps toward addressing highly polluting incidents with a new Super-Emitter Response Program; - Requiring that abandoned wells are subject to inspections until closure; and - Requiring that flares are properly operated to reduce emissions. I urge EPA to also consider ways to strengthen this rule, such as: - Ensuring that operators fully eliminate pollution from routine flaring; - Strengthening the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making them applicable to more tanks; and - Providing a clear pathway for community participation in the Super-Emitter Response Program by ensuring that local residents can access data and technologies to help reduce methane and other pollutants and that EPA maintains its commitment to environmental justice by ensuring that frontline communities most harmed by oil and gas sites can a", "b_text": "Dear EPA EPA, I am deeply concerned with the health impacts of air pollution and climate change on my family and my community. I am counting on a strong and comprehensive final methane rule to address the gaps in the rule proposed in 2021. I am encouraged to see that the latest methane proposal includes several improvements over the earlier version, such as - Strengthening leak detection and repair (LDAR) requirements, including requiring inspections at small wells with leak-prone equipment, which are responsible for roughly half of all the oil and gas methane emissions nationwide; - Maintaining zero-emitting pneumatic equipment requirements; - Taking steps toward addressing highly polluting incidents with a new Super-Emitter Response Program; - Requiring that abandoned wells are subject to inspections until closure; and - Requiring that flares are properly operated to reduce emissions. I urge EPA to also consider ways to strengthen this rule, such as: - Ensuring that operators fully eliminate pollution from routine flaring; - Strengthening the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making them applicable to more tanks; and - Providing a clear pathway for community participation in the Super-Emitter Response Program by ensuring that local residents can access data and technologies to help reduce methane and other pollutants and that EPA maintains its commitment to environmental justice by ensuring that frontline communities most harmed by oil and gas sites can a"}
{"pair": 135, "band": "0.99-1.01", "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0245", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0246", "a_text": "With increased regulations the price increases exponentially. This &ldquo;tax&rdquo; hurts every ones pocket, especially the poor . When will the Democrats quit claiming to protect the poor! They are breaking the backs of everyone!", "b_text": "With increased regulations the price increases exponentially. This &ldquo;tax&rdquo; hurts every ones pocket, especially the poor . When will the Democrats quit claiming to protect the poor! They are breaking the backs of everyone!"}
